/* generated by tools/gen-sample.js - do not edit. At most 2% of each level's lessons and questions. */
window.PQ_SAMPLE = {"igcse":{"name":"IGCSE Computer Science","code":"0478 / 0984","section":"Section 1.2 - Data storage: text, sound and images","lessons":[{"id":"1.2.1","code":"1.2.1","title":"Representing text: ASCII and Unicode","video":null,"concept":"<p>A computer stores text by giving every character (letter, digit, symbol) its own <b>binary code</b>. The list of characters and their codes is called a <b>character set</b>.</p><ul><li><b>ASCII</b> (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) uses 7 bits per character, so it has 128 different characters: English letters, digits and common symbols.</li><li><b>Unicode</b> uses more bits per character (for example 16 or 32), so it can store a <b>far greater range of characters</b>: many different languages, symbols and <b>emojis</b>.</li></ul><p>The trade-off: Unicode needs <b>more bits per character</b>, so text takes more storage.</p>","example":"<p>In ASCII the letter <b>A</b> has the code 65, <b>B</b> is 66, <b>C</b> is 67 and so on. The lower-case <b>a</b> is 97.</p><p>65 in 7-bit binary is <code>1000001</code>.</p><p>An emoji or a Chinese character cannot be stored in ASCII, because there are only 128 codes. Unicode has enough codes for them.</p>","checks":[{"q":"In ASCII the letter A has the code 65. What is the ASCII code of the letter D?","options":["66","67","69","68"],"correct":3,"explain":"A = 65, B = 66, C = 67, D = 68."},{"q":"Which statement correctly compares Unicode with ASCII?","options":["Unicode uses fewer bits per character than ASCII does","ASCII can store emojis and other languages but Unicode cannot","Unicode has more characters but needs more bits for each","Unicode and ASCII use exactly the same number of bits per character"],"correct":2,"explain":"Unicode has a far greater range of characters (languages, symbols and emojis) but uses more bits for each one."}]},{"id":"1.2.2","code":"1.2.2","title":"Representing sound","video":null,"concept":"<p>Sound is a continuous wave. To store it, the computer <b>samples</b> the wave: it measures the height of the wave at regular intervals and stores each measurement as a binary number.</p><ul><li><b>Sample rate</b> = the number of samples taken in <b>one second</b> (measured in hertz, Hz).</li><li><b>Sample resolution</b> = the number of <b>bits per sample</b>.</li></ul><p>If the sample rate and the sample resolution <b>increase</b>, the recording is a more <b>accurate</b> copy of the original sound, but the <b>file size also increases</b>.</p>","example":"<p>A CD recording uses a sample rate of 44 100 Hz (44 100 samples every second) and a sample resolution of 16 bits.</p><p>A phone call uses a much lower sample rate. The sound is less accurate, but the file is much smaller.</p><p>Doubling the sample rate means twice as many samples every second, so the file is twice as large.</p>","checks":[{"q":"What is meant by the sample rate of a sound recording?","options":["The number of bits that are used for each sample","The number of samples taken in one second","The length of the whole recording in seconds","The loudness of every sample that is taken"],"correct":1,"explain":"The sample rate is the number of samples taken per second. The number of bits per sample is the sample resolution."},{"q":"A sound is re-recorded with a higher sample resolution. What is the effect?","options":["The recording is more accurate and the file size increases","The recording is less accurate and the file size decreases","The recording is more accurate and the file size decreases","The recording is less accurate and the file size increases"],"correct":0,"explain":"More bits per sample gives a more accurate recording, and it also makes the file larger."}]},{"id":"1.2.3","code":"1.2.3","title":"Representing images","video":null,"concept":"<p>An image is a grid of tiny squares called <b>pixels</b>. Each pixel's colour is stored as a binary number.</p><ul><li><b>Resolution</b> = the number of pixels in the image (for example 800 &times; 600).</li><li><b>Colour depth</b> = the number of <b>bits used for each pixel</b>. With a colour depth of <i>n</i> bits an image can use 2<sup><i>n</i></sup> different colours.</li></ul><p>If the resolution and the colour depth <b>increase</b>, the <b>quality</b> of the image gets better but the <b>file size</b> also increases.</p>","example":"<table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Colour depth</th><th>Number of colours</th></tr><tr><td>1 bit</td><td>2 (for example black and white)</td></tr><tr><td>2 bits</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>8 bits</td><td>256</td></tr><tr><td>24 bits</td><td>16 777 216</td></tr></table><p>A picture with more pixels shows finer detail. A picture with a higher colour depth shows smoother shades of colour. Both need more bits to store.</p>","checks":[{"q":"An image has a colour depth of 4 bits. How many different colours can each pixel show?","options":["4","8","16","32"],"correct":2,"explain":"With 4 bits there are 2 x 2 x 2 x 2 = 16 different patterns, so 16 colours."},{"q":"A photograph's resolution and colour depth are both increased. What happens to its quality and its file size?","options":["Quality increases and file size decreases","Quality decreases and file size decreases","Quality decreases and file size increases","Quality increases and file size increases"],"correct":3,"explain":"A higher resolution and colour depth give a better quality image, but more bits are needed so the file is larger."}]}],"questions":[{"type":"mcq","q":"What is meant by the term 'character set'?","options":["A defined list of characters recognised by a computer, where each character is mapped to a unique binary code","Any list of symbols a person can draw by hand","The total number of files and folders a computer's hard drive can physically store before running out of space","A list of fonts installed on a computer"],"correct":0,"explain":"A character set maps every recognised character to a unique binary code so computers can store and process text.","topic":"ASCII vs Unicode"},{"type":"mcq","q":"Which statement correctly describes a difference between ASCII and Unicode?","options":["ASCII uses fewer bits per character and has a smaller range of characters; Unicode uses more bits per character and can represent many languages and emojis","ASCII can represent every global language on Earth, while Unicode is strictly and permanently limited to English-language characters only","ASCII and Unicode both use exactly 8 bits per character, so they always represent exactly the same number of symbols","Unicode is actually an audio compression format, while ASCII is only ever used for storing digital images"],"correct":0,"explain":"ASCII uses fewer bits per character, so its range of characters is smaller. Unicode uses more bits per character, so it can represent characters from many different languages and also emojis.","topic":"ASCII vs Unicode"},{"type":"mcq","q":"In ASCII, 'A' has the denary code 65. What is the denary code for 'D'?","options":["68","67","69","100"],"correct":0,"explain":"ASCII codes run consecutively through the alphabet: A=65, B=66, C=67, D=68.","topic":"ASCII vs Unicode"},{"type":"mcq","q":"How is an analogue sound wave converted into digital form?","options":["The sound wave is sampled at regular intervals, and each sample is stored as a binary value","The frequency of the wave is permanently removed and replaced entirely with complete silence throughout","The wave is converted directly into ASCII text characters","The wave is compressed first, and only then sampled"],"correct":0,"explain":"The sound wave is sampled at regular intervals, and each sample is then stored as a binary value.","topic":"Sound Sampling"},{"type":"mcq","q":"What happens when the sample resolution used to record sound is increased?","options":["Sound quality improves, but file size grows since more bits are needed to store each sample","Sound quality gets worse, but file size decreases","Neither sound quality nor file size is ever affected by changing the sample resolution at all","File size decreases while sound quality improves"],"correct":0,"explain":"More bits per sample capture amplitude more precisely (better quality), but each sample takes up more storage, so file size grows.","topic":"Sound Sampling"},{"type":"mcq","q":"What is a pixel?","options":["The smallest single component (picture element) of a digital bitmap image","A specialised unit used only to measure the analogue sound sample rate","The file extension used for compressed images","A specialised technical unit used only to measure how many colours a monitor can display at once"],"correct":0,"explain":"A pixel ('picture element') is the smallest individual component that makes up a digital bitmap image.","topic":"Pixels & Colour Depth"},{"type":"mcq","q":"An image has a colour depth of 8 bits. What is the maximum number of unique colours it can represent?","options":["256","128","64","16"],"correct":0,"explain":"With n bits of colour depth, the maximum number of colours is 2^n. Here, 2^8 = 256.","topic":"Pixels & Colour Depth"},{"type":"mcq","q":"Which two features of a bitmap image affect the size of its file?","options":["The make of the camera and the lens used","The image's resolution and its colour depth","The brightness of the screen it is viewed on","The date the file was last printed"],"correct":1,"explain":"A bitmap's file size depends on how many pixels it contains (its resolution) and how many bits are used to store the colour of each pixel (its colour depth).","topic":"Pixels & Colour Depth"}]},"as":{"name":"AS Level Computer Science","code":"9618","section":"Section 1.3 - Compression","lessons":[{"id":"1.3.1a","code":"1.3.1","title":"Why compress files? Lossy and lossless compression","video":null,"concept":"<p><b>Compression</b> makes a file smaller. A smaller file <b>takes less storage space</b>, is <b>faster to upload and download</b>, uses <b>less bandwidth</b> and less of a user's data allowance.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th></th><th>Lossless compression</th><th>Lossy compression</th></tr><tr><td>What happens</td><td>data is stored in a shorter way; <b>nothing is lost</b></td><td>some data is <b>permanently removed</b></td></tr><tr><td>Original recovered?</td><td>Yes, exactly (rebuilt perfectly)</td><td>No: the original cannot be re-created</td></tr><tr><td>File size</td><td>smaller</td><td>much smaller (a larger decrease than lossless)</td></tr><tr><td>Used for</td><td>text, program files, spreadsheets, some images (e.g. RLE)</td><td>photographs, music and video, where small changes are not noticed</td></tr></table><p><b>Run-length encoding (RLE)</b> is a lossless method: a run of consecutive identical items is stored as <b>the item and the number of times it repeats</b>. <code>WWWWWBB</code> becomes <code>W5 B2</code>.</p><p>Ways to reduce a bitmap file: lower the <b>colour (bit) depth</b> or the <b>image resolution</b>, or compress it.</p>","example":"<p><b>Why lossy suits a photograph:</b> the data removed (small differences in shade and detail) is usually not noticed by the human eye, and the file gets much smaller.</p><p><b>Why lossless suits a program file:</b> losing even one bit would make the program fail, so every bit must be recovered.</p><p><b>Estimate of a photograph 4000 &times; 3000 pixels at 4 bytes per pixel:</b> 4000 &times; 3000 &times; 4 = 48 000 000 bytes = <b>48 MB</b> (decimal prefix, as asked in megabytes).</p>","checks":[{"q":"Which type of compression permanently removes some data?","options":["Lossy","Lossless","RLE","Binary"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lossy compression loses data that cannot be recovered."},{"q":"A program file is compressed. Which method must be used?","options":["Lossless","Lossy","Either, it does not matter","Neither"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every bit of a program is needed."},{"q":"What is the run-length encoding of AAAABBBCC?","options":["A4 B3 C2","A3 B3 C3","4A 3B","ABC"],"correct":0,"explain":"Four As, three Bs, two Cs."},{"type":"text","q":"Give one benefit of compressing a photograph before uploading it to a web server. [1]","groups":[["less storage","smaller file","less space","faster to upload","faster to download","less bandwidth","less data","quicker","takes up less"]],"need":1,"model":"The file takes less storage space, uploads and downloads faster, uses less bandwidth or less of the data allowance.","explain":"The file takes less storage space, uploads and downloads faster, uses less bandwidth or less of the data allowance."}]},{"id":"1.3.1b","code":"1.3.1","title":"Compressing images, sound and text","video":null,"concept":"<p>Different files are compressed in different ways, and the exam asks you to <b>describe</b> how.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>File</th><th>Lossless method</th><th>Lossy method</th></tr><tr><td>Bitmap image</td><td><b>RLE</b>: store each colour and the number of times it appears in a row</td><td>reduce the colour depth or the resolution; remove detail the eye does not notice</td></tr><tr><td>Sound</td><td>RLE (for repeated values); or record only the <b>changes</b> in amplitude, each needing fewer bits than a whole value; or reduce the range of values to those actually used</td><td><b>reduce the sampling rate</b> (fewer samples per second); <b>reduce the sampling resolution</b> (fewer bits per sample); remove sounds outside the range of human hearing</td></tr><tr><td>Text / vector</td><td>RLE or storing common patterns once in a table</td><td>not suitable: removing letters would change the meaning</td></tr></table><p>Lossy compression gives a bigger saving but <b>cannot be undone</b>: reducing the sampling rate means there are fewer samples per second, so the original sound cannot be re-created.</p>","example":"<p>The first row of a bitmap is <code>R R W W W W</code>. RLE stores <code>R2 W4</code>: 2 values instead of 6.</p><p><b>Sound:</b> instead of storing the values 120, 121, 123, 124 in full (each needing 8 bits), store 120 followed by the changes +1, +2, +1 (each needing only a couple of bits). Lossless: the original values can be rebuilt exactly.</p><p><b>Mark scheme tip:</b> 'max 2 for image and max 2 for sound': answer <b>both</b> parts, and do not use the same method for both unless the question allows it.</p>","checks":[{"q":"Which lossless method stores a colour and the number of consecutive times it appears?","options":["Run-length encoding","Reducing the colour depth","Reducing the sampling rate","Deleting the file header"],"correct":0,"explain":"That is the definition of RLE."},{"q":"Reducing the sampling rate of a sound file is:","options":["Lossy compression","Lossless compression","Encryption","Run-length encoding"],"correct":0,"explain":"Data is permanently lost, so the original cannot be re-created."},{"q":"A sound is stored as the first value followed by the change from each value to the next. This is:","options":["Lossless compression","Lossy compression","Not compression","Encryption"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every original value can be rebuilt exactly."},{"type":"text","q":"Explain how lossless compression can be used for a bitmap image. [2]","groups":[["rle","run length"],["consecutive","same colour","repeated","in a row"],["number of times","count","how many","occurrences"]],"need":2,"model":"Use run-length encoding: store each colour and the number of times it appears consecutively.","explain":"Use run-length encoding: store each colour and the number of times it appears consecutively."}]}],"questions":[{"type":"paper","topic":"Compression","marks":3,"prompt":"A music file is compressed before it is uploaded.\n(a) State one benefit of compressing the file. [1]\n(b) The compression is lossy. Describe what happens to the data when lossy compression is used. [2]","markPoints":[{"text":"(a) Smaller file / less storage space / faster to upload or download / less bandwidth","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["smaller","less storage","less space","faster","quicker","less bandwidth","less data","takes up less"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"(b) Some data / detail is permanently removed","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["remove","removed","discard","discarded","lost","loses","deleted","permanent","less important"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"(b) The original file cannot be restored exactly / quality is reduced","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["cannot be restored","can't be restored","cannot be recovered","not be restored","original cannot","can not be","lower quality","quality is reduced","quality decreases","reduced quality","worse quality","not the same as the original","irreversible"]],"needCount":1}}],"explanation":"Compression makes the file smaller, so it needs less storage and uploads faster. Lossy compression permanently removes some data (usually the detail people notice least), so the original cannot be restored exactly."}]},"a2":{"name":"A Level (A2) Computer Science","code":"9618","section":"Section 13.2 - File organisation and access","lessons":[{"id":"13.2a","code":"13.2.1","title":"Serial and sequential file organisation","video":null,"concept":"<p>Data is stored in files as <b>records</b>. <b>File organisation</b> is how the records are physically arranged in the file.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th></th><th>Serial</th><th>Sequential</th></tr><tr><td>Order of records</td><td>One after another in the order they were <b>added</b> (chronological)</td><td>One after another in a <b>given order</b>, usually by a <b>key field</b></td></tr><tr><td>Adding a record</td><td><b>Appended</b> at the end</td><td><b>Inserted in the correct place</b> to keep the order</td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Temporary files, transaction logs, batch processing, backups</td><td>Master files such as payroll, billing, customer accounts</td></tr></table><p>A <b>key field</b> is a field whose value is unique for every record and identifies it, for example a customer number.</p>","example":"<p>A gas company stores customer meter readings in the order they arrive. This is a <b>serial</b> file (chronological). At the end of the month a program sorts them by customer number and uses them to update the customer master file, which is <b>sequential</b> (ordered by customer number).</p><p>If Customer 5 is added to a sequential file that holds customers 1, 2, 3, 4, 7 and 8, the record has to go between 4 and 7.</p><p><b>Exam link:</b> mark schemes award marks for <i>records stored one after another in chronological order</i> (serial) and <i>in a given order based on a key field</i> (sequential). Chronological order is not correct for sequential.</p>","checks":[{"q":"In a <b>serial</b> file, how are records stored?","options":["One after another in the order they were added","In order of the key field","At a position calculated from the key field","In a random order chosen by the user"],"correct":0,"explain":"Serial: chronological order of arrival, with new records appended at the end."},{"q":"In a <b>sequential</b> file the records are stored one after another ...","options":["in a given order, usually based on a key field","in the order they arrived","at positions found by hashing","in no particular order"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sequential: ordered by a key field, so new records are inserted in the right place."},{"type":"text","q":"Describe serial file organisation. [2]","groups":[["one after another","next to each other","linear","sequence","stored in order"],["order","chronological","order added","order they were added","order of arrival"],["end of the file","appended","added at the end"]],"need":2,"model":"Records are stored in a file one after another in chronological order (the order in which they were added), and new records are appended at the end of the file.","explain":"Records are stored in a file one after another in chronological order (the order in which they were added), and new records are appended at the end of the file."},{"q":"Which is the most suitable use of a sequential file?","options":["A payroll file processed in employee number order every month","Storing each customer meter reading as it arrives","Finding one booking instantly in an airline system","Storing a game's save data in any free space"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sequential files suit processing whole files in key order. Arrival-order logging is serial; instant lookup suits a random file."}]},{"id":"13.2b","code":"13.2.1","title":"Random file organisation","video":null,"concept":"<p>In <b>random file organisation</b> records are stored in <b>any available position</b> in the file. The position of a record is calculated by applying a <b>hashing algorithm</b> to its <b>key field</b>, so a record can be found directly without reading the others.</p><ul><li>New records go into an empty location (the one the hash function calculates).</li><li>Records are <b>not</b> in key order and not in the order they arrived.</li></ul><p><b>Typical uses:</b> booking systems (e.g. airline), operating system file management, video games, encryption and compression utilities: anything that needs fast access to <b>individual</b> records.</p>","example":"<p>An airline stores a seat booking under the booking reference. To find booking 3024 the system does not search the whole file: it applies the hash function to 3024 and goes to the calculated position.</p><p>This is why random organisation is chosen for <b>login files</b> and <b>booking systems</b>: lookups are quick and only one record is needed each time.</p>","checks":[{"q":"In a random file, where is a record stored?","options":["At a position calculated by applying a hashing algorithm to its key field","After the last record that was added","In key order with the lowest key first","At a position chosen by the user"],"correct":0,"explain":"The hash function turns the record key into an address."},{"q":"Which file organisation is most suitable for a booking system where individual records must be found quickly?","options":["Random","Serial","Sequential","Chronological"],"correct":0,"explain":"Random organisation allows direct access to a single record."},{"type":"text","q":"Describe how a record is stored in a random file. [3]","groups":[["any available","any position","no particular order","empty position","empty location","any free"],["record key","key field","key"],["hash","hashing","hash function","hashing algorithm"],["calculate","calculated","determine","address","location"]],"need":3,"model":"Records are stored in any available (empty) position. A hashing function is applied to the record key (key field) to calculate the location where the record is stored.","explain":"Records are stored in any available (empty) position. A hashing function is applied to the record key (key field) to calculate the location where the record is stored."}]},{"id":"13.2c","code":"13.2.2","title":"File access: sequential and direct","video":null,"concept":"<p><b>File access</b> is the method used to physically <b>find</b> a record in a file.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th></th><th>Sequential access</th><th>Direct access</th></tr><tr><td>How</td><td>Records are searched <b>one after another from the start of the file</b> until the record is found</td><td>A record is found <b>without reading the others</b></td></tr><tr><td>Used with</td><td><b>Serial</b> and <b>sequential</b> files</td><td><b>Sequential</b> files (using an <b>index</b> of key fields) and <b>random</b> files (using a <b>hash function</b>)</td></tr><tr><td>Search ends when</td><td>The record is found, the end of the file is reached, or (sequential file) the current key is <b>greater</b> than the key being searched</td><td>The calculated address is read and the key checked</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Processing <b>every</b> record (high hit rate), e.g. monthly bills or payroll</td><td>Finding or updating <b>one</b> record (low hit rate), e.g. changing a customer's phone number</td></tr></table>","example":"<p>A sequential file holds customers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 and 8. A program searches for Customer 6 using <b>sequential access</b>. It reads 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, then reaches 7, which is greater than 6, so it stops: Customer 6 is not in the file. The records 7 and 8 never needed reading.</p><p>In a serial file the whole file would have to be read, because the records are in no key order.</p><p><b>Exam link:</b> in a past paper, the mark points for searching a sequential file were: each record is searched one after another / from the physical start of the file / using the record key / until the record is found, or the key being searched has been exceeded, or the end of the file is reached.</p>","checks":[{"q":"Which pairing of file organisation and access is correct?","options":["Random file and direct access","Serial file and direct access","Random file and sequential access only","Serial file and index lookup"],"correct":0,"explain":"Random files use direct access (hashing). Serial files can only be read sequentially. Sequential files can use sequential access or direct access using an index."},{"q":"A sequential file is searched for the key 40. The records read are 10, 20, 30, 50. What does the program conclude?","options":["Record 40 is not in the file","Record 40 is the last record","Record 40 is the next record","The file is corrupt"],"correct":0,"explain":"The key 50 is greater than 40, so 40 would have been found earlier: it does not exist."},{"q":"Which task suits sequential access best?","options":["Printing every customer's bill at the end of the month","Updating the address of one customer","Finding one flight booking","Checking one password"],"correct":0,"explain":"Processing every record (high hit rate) is efficient with sequential access."},{"type":"text","q":"Describe how a record is found in a serial file using sequential access. [3]","groups":[["each record","one after another","one at a time","linearly","in turn"],["start","beginning","first record","physical start"],["record key","key field","key","compare","check"],["until","found","end of the file"]],"need":3,"model":"Sequential access starts at the physical start of the file and checks each record, one after another, comparing the record key, until the record is found or the end of the file is reached.","explain":"Sequential access starts at the physical start of the file and checks each record, one after another, comparing the record key, until the record is found or the end of the file is reached."}]},{"id":"13.2d","code":"13.2.3","title":"Hashing algorithms","video":null,"concept":"<p>A <b>hashing algorithm</b> is a formula applied to a record's <b>key field</b> that gives the <b>address</b> where the record is stored. A simple one uses <b>MOD</b>:</p><pre class='ln-pre'>Address = StartAddress + (Key MOD NumberOfRecords) * RecordSize</pre><ul><li>Key MOD NumberOfRecords always gives a value from 0 to NumberOfRecords − 1, so the address stays inside the file.</li><li>RecordSize is the number of locations each record uses.</li></ul><p>To <b>read</b> a record, use the same formula, go to the address, then <b>check that the key stored there matches</b> the key being searched.</p>","example":"<p>A file starts at address 500 and has space for 1000 records, each using 5 locations. Key fields are from 1 to 9999.</p><pre class='ln-pre'>Address = 500 + (Key MOD 1000) * 5</pre><p>Key 9354: 9354 MOD 1000 = 354, so address = 500 + 354 × 5 = <b>2270</b>.</p><p>Another simple file has space for 2000 records, starts at address 0 and each record takes one location. Key 3024: 3024 MOD 2000 = 1024, so the record goes to address <b>1024</b>.</p>","checks":[{"q":"A random file starts at address 0. It has space for 200 records of one location each. The hash function is <code>Key MOD 200</code>. Where is the record with key 1337 stored?","options":["137","1337","200","7"],"correct":0,"explain":"1337 MOD 200 = 137 (1337 = 6 × 200 + 137), so the address is 0 + 137 = 137."},{"q":"A file starts at address 100. It has space for 500 records. Each record uses 4 locations. The hash function is <code>100 + (Key MOD 500) * 4</code>. What is the address for key 1234?","options":["1036","1234","934","936"],"correct":0,"explain":"1234 MOD 500 = 234. 234 × 4 = 936. 100 + 936 = 1036."},{"q":"Why must the key stored at the calculated address be checked when a record is read?","options":["Different keys can give the same address","The hash function changes each time it is used","The file is sorted by key","The address might be negative"],"correct":0,"explain":"Two different keys can hash to the same address (a collision), so the stored key must match the key being searched."}]},{"id":"13.2e","code":"13.2.3","title":"Collisions: open and closed hashing","video":null,"concept":"<p>A <b>collision</b> happens when two different keys give the <b>same address</b>. For example, with <code>Key MOD 2000</code>, keys 3024 and 5024 both give 1024. A collision-handling method is needed.</p><table class='ln-table'><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th></tr><tr><td><b>Open hashing</b> (open addressing, linear probing)</td><td>The record is stored in the <b>next free location</b> after the calculated address</td></tr><tr><td><b>Closed hashing</b> (overflow area)</td><td>An <b>overflow area</b> is set up, and the record is stored in the next free space <b>in the overflow area</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Finding</b> a record: calculate the address and read the record there. If its key does not match, <b>search on</b>: the following locations (open hash) or the overflow area (closed hash) until the key is found.</p>","example":"<p>Using <code>Key MOD 2000</code> with open hashing: key 3024 is stored at 1024. Later, key 5024 also gives 1024, which is taken, so the record is stored at the next free location, <b>1025</b>.</p><p>To read key 5024 the program goes to 1024, sees key 3024 (no match), moves to 1025 and finds 5024.</p><p><b>Exam link:</b> past-paper mark points for finding a record in a random file: direct access is used; a hash function is applied to the key; if the record at that location matches it is retrieved; otherwise a collision strategy is used, e.g. the following records are searched linearly (linear probing).</p>","checks":[{"q":"What is a collision in hashing?","options":["Two different keys produce the same address","The file is full","A key is not found","The hash function produces a negative number"],"correct":0,"explain":"A collision is when two keys hash to the same location."},{"q":"In <b>open</b> hashing, where is a record placed if its calculated address is already used?","options":["In the next free location after that address","In a separate overflow area","At the start of the file","It is discarded"],"correct":0,"explain":"Open hashing uses the next free space; closed hashing uses an overflow area."},{"type":"text","q":"Describe how a record is retrieved from a random file that uses a hashing algorithm. [4]","groups":[["hash","hashing","hash function"],["key field","record key","key"],["address","location","position","calculate"],["match","compare","check","found"],["collision","not found","does not match","next","overflow","linear","probing"]],"need":4,"model":"A hashing function is applied to the record key to calculate the address. The record at that location is read and its key is compared. If it matches it is retrieved. If not found (a collision) the following locations are searched linearly (open hash) or the overflow area is searched (closed hash).","explain":"A hashing function is applied to the record key to calculate the address. The record at that location is read and its key is compared. If it matches it is retrieved. If not found (a collision) the following locations are searched linearly (open hash) or the overflow area is searched (closed hash)."}]}],"questions":[{"type":"paper","topic":"File organisation and access","marks":3,"prompt":"A bank stores customer records in a file.\n(a) Describe how records are stored in a serial file. [1]\n(b) Explain why a sequential file is quicker to search than a serial file when the records are in key order. [2]","markPoints":[{"text":"(a) Records are stored one after another in the order they were added (no ordering by key)","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["order they were added","order added","chronological","added in order","one after another","no order","unordered","not in order","as they are added","time order","order they are created","order they were created"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"(b) Sequential records are held in order of a key field","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["key field","key order","sorted","in order of","ordered by","order of the key","ordered"]],"needCount":1}},{"text":"(b) So the search can stop once the key is passed / does not have to check every record","marks":1,"match":{"type":"keywords","groups":[["stop","passed","bigger","greater","larger","not have to","does not need to","doesn't need to","fewer","not every","not all","without checking"]],"needCount":1}}],"explanation":"A serial file stores records in the order they were added, so a search may have to read every record. A sequential file is kept in key order, so a search can stop as soon as it reaches a key larger than the one wanted."}]}};
